# RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) — Explained in Detail

## The Problem RAG Solves

LLMs have three fundamental limitations:

1. **Knowledge cutoff** — the model only knows what was in its training data. It knows nothing about your company's internal documents, your product catalog, or anything that happened after training.
2. **No private data** — it has never seen your order database, your HR policies, your API docs.
3. **Hallucination** — when asked about things it doesn't know, an LLM will often confidently make something up rather than say "I don't know."

You *could* fine-tune a model on your data, but that's expensive, slow, hard to update (retrain every time a document changes), and doesn't actually fix hallucination well.

RAG takes a different approach: **instead of teaching the model your data, you look up the relevant data at question-time and paste it into the prompt.** The model then answers *grounded* in that provided context.

The name literally describes the flow: **Retrieval** (find relevant documents) → **Augmented** (inject them into the prompt) → **Generation** (LLM writes the answer).

---

## The Core Insight: Embeddings & Semantic Search

The magic that makes retrieval work is **embeddings**.

An embedding model converts text into a vector — a list of numbers (e.g., 1,536 floats) — that represents the text's *meaning*. Texts with similar meanings end up close together in that vector space:

```
"How do I reset my password?"     → [0.12, -0.83, 0.44, ...]
"Steps to recover account access" → [0.14, -0.79, 0.41, ...]   ← very close
"Best pizza recipe"               → [-0.67, 0.22, -0.91, ...]  ← far away
```

This means you can find relevant documents by **similarity of meaning**, not keyword matching. "My login isn't working" will match a document titled "Password Recovery Procedure" even though they share zero keywords. That's the leap over classic full-text search.

Vectors are stored in a **vector store/database** — PGVector (Postgres extension), Chroma, Milvus, Weaviate, Redis, Elasticsearch, Pinecone, etc. — which can efficiently answer "give me the K vectors nearest to this query vector" (typically via cosine similarity and ANN indexes like HNSW).

