## The Two Phases of RAG

### Phase 1: Ingestion (offline, ahead of time)

This is the ETL pipeline that prepares your knowledge base:

```
Documents (PDF, Word, HTML, Confluence, DB rows...)
   │
   ▼  1. READ — extract raw text
   │
   ▼  2. SPLIT — chunk into pieces (e.g., 300–1000 tokens each)
   │
   ▼  3. ENRICH — attach metadata (source, date, department, ACLs)
   │
   ▼  4. EMBED — convert each chunk to a vector
   │
   ▼  5. STORE — write (vector + text + metadata) to vector store
```

**Why chunking?** Two reasons:
- The context window is finite — you can't paste a 200-page manual into a prompt.
- Retrieval precision — if a whole document is one vector, the vector is a mushy average of many topics. Small, focused chunks give sharp matches.

Chunking is more art than science. Common strategies: fixed-size with overlap (e.g., 800 tokens, 100 overlap so ideas that straddle a boundary aren't lost), sentence/paragraph-aware splitting, or structure-aware splitting (by headings, sections). Bad chunking is the #1 cause of bad RAG quality.

In Spring AI, this maps directly onto the API:

```java
// Ingestion pipeline
var reader   = new PagePdfDocumentReader(pdfResource);        // 1. read
var splitter = TokenTextSplitter.builder()
                   .withChunkSize(800)
                   .build();                                   // 2. split

List<Document> chunks = splitter.apply(reader.get());
chunks.forEach(d -> d.getMetadata().put("source", "hr-handbook")); // 3. enrich

vectorStore.add(chunks);   // 4 + 5: embeds via EmbeddingModel and stores
```

### Phase 2: Retrieval & Generation (online, per user question)

```
User: "How many vacation days do I get after 5 years?"
   │
   ▼  1. EMBED the question with the same embedding model
   │
   ▼  2. SEARCH the vector store → top-K most similar chunks
   │        (optionally filtered by metadata: department = HR)
   │
   ▼  3. AUGMENT — build a prompt like:
   │
   │     "Answer using ONLY the following context.
   │      If the answer isn't in the context, say you don't know.
   │
   │      Context:
   │      [chunk 1: 'Employees with 5+ years of service accrue 25 days...']
   │      [chunk 2: '...vacation policy revision 2024...']
   │
   │      Question: How many vacation days do I get after 5 years?"
   │
   ▼  4. GENERATE — LLM answers grounded in the chunks
   │
   ▼  "After 5 years of service you're entitled to 25 vacation days,
      per the HR handbook (revised 2024)."
```

In Spring AI this whole phase is one advisor — this is what the `QuestionAnswerAdvisor` in my first answer was doing:

```java
ChatClient chatClient = builder
    .defaultAdvisors(
        QuestionAnswerAdvisor.builder(vectorStore)
            .searchRequest(SearchRequest.builder()
                .topK(5)                       // retrieve 5 chunks
                .similarityThreshold(0.7)      // ignore weak matches
                .build())
            .build())
    .build();

// Now every prompt is automatically augmented with retrieved context
String answer = chatClient.prompt()
    .user("How many vacation days do I get after 5 years?")
    .call()
    .content();
```

The advisor intercepts the request, does the embed → search → prompt-stuffing dance, and forwards the augmented prompt to the model. Your calling code doesn't change at all.

